.TH GCS 1
.SH NAME
gcs \- Google Cloud Storage from an instance's own credential
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gcs
[
.BI -A " addr"
]
[
.BI -t " tokenfile"
]
.B ls
.I bucket
[
.I prefix
]
.br
.B gcs
[ ... ]
.B get
.I bucket object
.br
.B gcs
[ ... ]
.B put
.I bucket object file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gcs
lists, reads and writes objects in a Google Cloud Storage bucket.
.B Get
writes the object to standard output;
.B put
sends the contents of
.I file
and reports the size.
.PP
The credential is the one
.IR cloudfs (4)
already serves.
.I Gcs
reads
.B /cloud/token
\(em the metadata service's reply, from which it takes
.B access_token
\(em and sends it as an
.B Authorization: Bearer
header over TLS.  Nothing is stored on the machine and nothing is
signed, which is why this is the route to prefer on an instance.  A
file holding only the token is accepted too.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BI -A " addr"
Dial
.I addr
rather than resolving
.BR storage.googleapis.com .
The host is still what is named in SNI and required of the
certificate.  Needed because nothing in this tree resolves names yet.
.TP
.BI -t " tokenfile"
Read the credential from
.I tokenfile
instead of
.BR /cloud/token .
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
.EX
cloudinit
mount {cloudfs} /cloud
gcs ls my-bucket
gcs get my-bucket hello.txt
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gcs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cloudfs (4),
.IR cloudinit (1),
.IR httpclient (2),
.IR awsv4 (2)
.SH BUGS
The instance's service account must have a storage scope; the default
Compute Engine account is read-only unless the instance was created
otherwise, and a
.B put
then fails with 403.
.PP
The XML API is used, and the listing is scanned for
.B <Key>
elements rather than parsed.
.PP
Continuation is implemented: a reply carrying
.B <IsTruncated>true</IsTruncated>
is followed by another request with the
.B <NextContinuationToken>
it returns, percent-encoded, until the listing ends.  A truncated reply
with no token raises an error rather than returning a short listing as
though it were whole \(em which is what this did before, and a listing
that is silently short is the worst way for one to be wrong, because
the output looks like a complete answer.
.PP
The continuation
.I loop
has not been run against a bucket with more than one page of objects;
that needs an instance with storage credentials.  The parsing and the
encoding it depends on are tested: a token containing
.BR = ,
.B +
and
.B /
survives extraction and comes back as
.BR %3D ,
.B %2B
and
.BR %2F ,
and a final page with
.B <IsTruncated>false</IsTruncated>
ends the loop.
.PP
The other route is not wired up:
.B storage.googleapis.com
also speaks S3's API with SigV4 and interoperability keys, which
.IR awsv4 (2)
is in the tree for, and which is what a machine outside Google \(em or
one talking to S3 itself \(em would need.
